  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nIssued at 211 PM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nA ridge axis will be firmly in place for our Saturday with dam \nheights above 594 decameters for a good portion of the area. This \nhigh pressure will also suppress the monsoonal moisture flow and keep \nit well to our south. The sinking and downsloping air should help \ndry and heat up the afternoon atmosphere. Expect high temperatures \nranging from the mid to upper 80s in the interior basins and foot \nhills to low to upper 90s north and east of the Laramie Range. Some \nlocations in Converse and Niobrara Counties could reach or exceed \nrecord daily high temperatures. Another area of concern with the \nexcessive heat is how dry it will be. Minimum afternoon humidity \nvalues are progged to be in the lower 20s to the low teens and \nsingle digits for the northern and western fire zones. Winds appear \ntame and are expected to preclude red flag conditions. A few \nisolated storms are possible in the mountains and very southern \nportions of the CWA during the afternoon hours with just enough \nmoisture to kick off high based thunderstorms. Would not expect \nthese storms to last long or produce much rainfall.\n\nSunday and Monday still has the ridge axis over the area. There may \nbe a few weak shortwave troughs that could flatten the ridge \nslightly. The probability of a few more afternoon thunderstorms \ncould result, but chances are low and storms would be dry. The main \nconcern is for the excessive heat Sunday. Again temperatures soar to \nthe upper 90s to mid 100s for many locations. The current forecast \nis for daily record highs to be broken or tied. Winds do increase \nalso Sunday with the increasing pressure gradients influenced by the \nshortwave troughs. RH values plummet into the single digits over a \nlarge part of the area and overnight recoveries are likely to be \npoor. Therefore it is probable red flag products are issued if these \nconditions hold in the forecast.\n\nMonday evening through the rest of the forecast period has ensemble \nsupport of a trough clipping the border and extending south through \nour area. This would suppress the ridge into the deep southwest and \nallow more seasonable conditions to return. Moisture remains a \nscarce commodity, but perhaps there is enough along with enhanced \nsynoptic lift to trigger some showers and storms, especially over \nthe ranges. Hopefully a cold front associated with this trough can \nprovide some relief from the heat and increase surface humidity.",
